          ],
          "high": 4,
          "low": -2,
          "param": "angle_guard"
        },
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 30,
          "low": 4,
          "param": "n_iter"
        },
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 28,
          "low": 8,
          "param": "data_width"
        }
      ],
      "why": "Full per-stage pipeline gives 1 result/cycle, so throughput_msps = Fmax. To hit >=1 MSPS we need Fmax >= 1 MHz, which is trivially met at sky130 typical corner for any sane width. The binding constraint is max_abs_err <= 2^-10, which depends only on data_width, n_iter, angle_guard, frac_guard, rounding. Since area is the sole selection objective and pipelined has the highest area (a register per stage), I want the search to find the minimum-area point that still satisfies the error constraint, and to map how accuracy_bits trades off against area as n_iter/data_width grow. Sweeping the full registry ranges lets Optuna find the exact knee where error just meets 2^-10 with minimal area, and also populate the accuracy_bits objective for hypervolume."
    },
    {
      "budget_share": 0.5,
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "ranges": [
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 4,
          "low": 0,
          "param": "frac_guard"
        },
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 4,
          "low": -2,
          "param": "angle_guard"
        },
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 30,
          "low": 4,
          "param": "n_iter"
        },
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 28,
          "low": 8,
          "param": "data_width"
        },
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 8,
          "low": 2,
          "param": "m"
        }
      ],
      "why": "pipelined_m registers every m stages, so it has fewer flip-flops and less area than pipelined for the same N, while still delivering 1 result/cycle (throughput = Fmax >= 1 MSPS easily met). The extra parameter m (2..8) lets the search trade area against latency and Fmax: larger m means fewer registers (lower area) but longer combinational chains per stage group (lower Fmax, though Fmax only needs to be >= 1 MHz here so this is unlikely to bind). This family is the most promising for the min-area selection rule because it can achieve the same accuracy as pipelined with strictly less area. Sweeping the full ranges including m lets Optuna find the global minimum-area feasible point and map the area-vs-accuracy trade-off curve for hypervolume."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "Both allowed families deliver 1 result/cycle, so the throughput constraint (>=1 MSPS) reduces to Fmax >= 1 MHz, which is trivially satisfied at sky130 typical corner for all reasonable widths and iteration counts. The binding constraint is max_abs_err <= 2^-10, which depends only on the accuracy parameters (data_width, n_iter, angle_guard, frac_guard, rounding) and is identical across all families. Since the selection rule is min area_um2, the key question is which family achieves the required accuracy with the least area. pipelined_m dominates pipelined on area for the same accuracy (fewer registers), so I expect the Pareto front to be dominated by pipelined_m points, with pipelined contributing only at the high-accuracy end if needed. I split the budget 50/50 between the two families: pipelined_m is the primary candidate for the minimum-area design, while pipelined provides a reference and may contribute distinct points on the accuracy_bits axis for hypervolume. Full registry ranges are used so Optuna can locate the exact error-constraint boundary and map the trade-off curve; the reserved front-mapping portion of the budget will then refine the Pareto front over these ranges. No families outside my assignment are used."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "next_plan": null,
  "rationale": "The feasible Pareto front is well-established with 7 distinct designs spanning area 33708-81046 um^2 and accuracy 14.3-19.2 bits. The minimum area design (pipelined_m, 33708 um^2) meets all constraints: throughput 22.7 MSPS >= 1 MSPS and max_abs_err 4.85e-05 <= 2^-10 (0.000976562). The front covers the trade-off space adequately for the hypervolume objective, and the selection rule (min area) clearly points to the 33708 um^2 design. Since this is the final round, stopping is appropriate."
}
